          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient with episodic wheeze shows a fifteen percent improvement in FEV1 after</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with episodic wheeze shows a fifteen percent improvement in FEV1 after a bronchodilator. Which obstructive airway condition is confirmed by this reversibility?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Emphysema</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Emphysema causes permanent alveolar wall destruction. The resulting airflow obstruction is fixed and does not improve with bronchodilators.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chronic bronchitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chronic bronchitis produces fixed obstruction from mucous gland hypertrophy and chronic airway inflammation. Bronchodilators do not reverse this significantly.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Asthma</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Asthma is defined by episodic, reversible bronchoconstriction. A ≥12% FEV1 improvement after a bronchodilator confirms reversibility and distinguishes it from the fixed obstruction of COPD and bronchiectasis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bronchiectasis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bronchiectasis involves permanent, irreversible bronchial dilation driven by the obstruction-infection cycle. Spirometric obstruction does not reverse with bronchodilators.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Reversibility on spirometry — at least a 12% improvement in FEV1 after a bronchodilator — is the hallmark of asthma. In COPD (emphysema and chronic bronchitis) and bronchiectasis, the obstruction is fixed and irreversible."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Reversibility on spirometry is the key feature that distinguishes asthma from COPD and bronchiectasis, where obstruction is fixed or permanent."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
