          "label": "<p>A patient with cavitary tuberculosis develops sudden massive haemoptysis. Which </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with cavitary tuberculosis develops sudden massive haemoptysis. Which pathological mechanism best explains this complication?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rupture of a Rasmussen aneurysm in the cavity wall</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Inflammation of the cavity wall erodes a pulmonary artery branch, forming a fusiform Rasmussen aneurysm. Its rupture releases arterial blood directly into the airway, causing massive haemoptysis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Erosion of a caseous granuloma into a pulmonary vein</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Erosion into a pulmonary vein seeds bacilli into the systemic bloodstream, causing miliary tuberculosis — haematogenous dissemination across organs, not localised haemoptysis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Development of secondary amyloidosis in the bronchial wall</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Secondary amyloidosis in tuberculosis deposits amyloid protein in the kidneys, liver, and spleen. It does not affect the bronchial vasculature or cause haemoptysis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Traction bronchiectasis from fibrotic healing</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Traction bronchiectasis results from fibrosis pulling on the airways. While it is a recognised TB complication, the acute mechanism of massive haemoptysis is arterial rupture from a Rasmussen aneurysm.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Massive haemoptysis in cavitary TB results from a Rasmussen aneurysm — a fusiform dilatation of a pulmonary artery branch eroded by cavity-wall inflammation. Its rupture releases arterial blood directly into the airway."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The Rasmussen aneurysm forms when cavity-wall inflammation erodes a pulmonary artery branch. Its rupture is one of the most dangerous complications of cavitary tuberculosis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
