          "label": "<p>A patient with long-standing silicosis develops pulmonary tuberculosis. Which me</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient with long-standing silicosis develops pulmonary tuberculosis. Which mechanism best explains this well-recognised association?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Silica particles directly stimulate mycobacterial replication</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Silica does not promote bacterial growth. Its pathogenic effect is on the host immune cells, not on the bacillus itself.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Progressive massive fibrosis traps mycobacteria in fibrotic tissue</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While progressive massive fibrosis distorts lung architecture, the mechanism linking silicosis to tuberculosis is immunological — impaired macrophage function — not mechanical trapping.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Whorled silicotic nodules create anaerobic niches favouring TB growth</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>M. tuberculosis is an obligate aerobe that thrives in high-oxygen environments like the lung apices. Anaerobic conditions would hinder, not favour, its growth.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Silica disables macrophage bactericidal function</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Silica uniquely impairs the killing capacity of alveolar macrophages, the very cells responsible for containing M. tuberculosis. This immunological defect explains silicotuberculosis as a recognised and serious combination.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The link between silicosis and tuberculosis is immunological: silica uniquely disables alveolar macrophage bactericidal function. Since macrophages are the primary defence against M. tuberculosis, their impairment leaves the patient vulnerable to infection."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Silica disables macrophage bactericidal function, removing the primary immune defence against M. tuberculosis and explaining why silicotuberculosis is a well-recognised entity."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
