          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which immunohistochemistry marker, when positive, supports a diagnosis of mesoth</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which immunohistochemistry marker, when positive, supports a diagnosis of mesothelioma over metastatic adenocarcinoma of the pleura?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>TTF-1</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>TTF-1 (thyroid transcription factor 1) is positive in lung adenocarcinoma, not mesothelioma. Its presence in a pleural tumour favours a metastatic origin from the lung.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Calretinin</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Calretinin is part of the mesothelioma panel (calretinin, WT1, D2-40, CK5/6). Its positivity, combined with TTF-1 and CEA negativity, identifies a pleural tumour as primary mesothelioma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CEA</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Carcinoembryonic antigen is positive in adenocarcinoma. Its presence in a pleural tumour points toward metastatic disease rather than primary mesothelioma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>MOC-31</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>MOC-31 is positive in metastatic adenocarcinoma and typically negative in mesothelioma. It helps rule out mesothelioma rather than confirm it.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Calretinin is the correct answer. The mesothelioma panel — calretinin, WT1, D2-40, and CK5/6 — is positive in primary mesothelioma. TTF-1, CEA, and MOC-31 are positive in metastatic adenocarcinoma. This distinction is the practical diagnostic tool for pleural tumours."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Calretinin positivity is one of the key markers in the mesothelioma IHC panel, distinguishing it from pleural metastases that show TTF-1, CEA, and MOC-31 positivity instead."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
